---
/**
 * The two vertical hairlines marking the page's left and right margins: the
 * outer edges of the first and twelfth columns of the layout grid.
 *
 * Purely decorative, so it is hidden from assistive technology and never
 * intercepts pointer events. Unlike the Shift+G development grid in
 * ColumnGrid.astro, this is part of the design and visible at every width
 * above the mobile breakpoint — on phones the margins are too narrow for the
 * lines to read as a grid, so they are dropped.
 *
 * The section it is dropped into must establish a positioning context, which
 * in practice means `position: relative`.
 *
 * Horizontal rules that cross these lines must be drawn inside the padding box
 * (e.g. a bottom-edge background gradient), not as CSS borders — borders sit
 * outside the absolute containing block, so the strokes would not share pixels.
 * Where both hairlines paint, the semi-transparent ink composites to a lighter
 * cross.
 */
---

<div class="vertical-edges" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<style>
  .vertical-edges {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Same hairline as the section rules. Stacked over a horizontal rule, the
     two paper-at-19% strokes composite to a brighter intersection. */
  .vertical-edges::before,
  .vertical-edges::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    width: var(--border-hairline-width);
    background-color: var(--border-hairline);
  }

  .vertical-edges::before {
    inset-inline-start: var(--grid-margin);
  }

  .vertical-edges::after {
    inset-inline-end: var(--grid-margin);
  }

  /* Mobile: no vertical rules. The margins collapse to ~30px here, so the two
     hairlines crowd the content instead of framing it. */
  @media (max-width: 699px) {
    .vertical-edges::before,
    .vertical-edges::after {
      content: none;
    }
  }
</style>
